---

<DocsLayout
  title="Enxame mode — save tokens with a swarm of z.ai workers"
  description="Enxame mode lets your OpenRouter model hand small, mechanical sub-tasks to up to 10 cheap z.ai GLM flash workers in parallel, so the expensive model only spends tokens on planning and hard reasoning."
  heading="enxame mode"
  lede="Enxame is Portuguese for swarm. Your OpenRouter model manages, and cheap z.ai workers do the busywork."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="idea">the idea</h2>
  <p>
    Coding agents spend many tokens on mechanical work: summarizing files, extracting
    fields, classifying items, and making small scoped edits. In enxame mode, your main
    OpenRouter model stays the <strong>manager</strong>. It keeps planning, hard reasoning,
    and final decisions, and fans the rest out to a swarm of fast GLM flash models on z.ai
    (<code>glm-4.7-flash</code>, falling back to <code>glm-4.5-flash</code>). Only short,
    structured results come back into the manager's context.
  </p>

  <h2 id="setup">set it up</h2>
  <pre><code>/enxame</code></pre>
  <p>Paste a z.ai API key, then use the menu:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">option</th><th scope="col">what it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Enable / Disable swarm</td><td>Turn the swarm on or off without removing the key.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mode</td><td>Choose <strong>Tool</strong>, <strong>Agent</strong>, or <strong>Both</strong> (the default).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Workers</td><td>Set the maximum number of workers running at once, from 1 to 10 (default 10).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Test connection</td><td>Send a quick request to check the key and model.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Replace / Remove account</td><td>Change or delete the stored z.ai key.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The key is stored in <code>~/.openclaude/config.json</code> with owner-only permissions.</p>

  <h2 id="modes">modes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Tool (Ferramenta)</strong>: the cheapest mode. The manager gets an
      <code>Enxame</code> tool that sends many <em>stateless</em> prompts in parallel. A
      worker sees only the prompt it was given, with no history, files, or tools.
      Results come back as a JSON array of <code>{'{id, ok, text}'}</code> in input order.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Agent (Agente)</strong>: the manager can launch <code>enxame-worker</code>
      sub-agents that run on z.ai <em>with tools</em>. They can read and search files, run
      a command, or make a small edit. Workers never spawn more agents and don't load
      project memory files.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Both (Ambos)</strong>: both options are available, and the manager picks per task.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="good-tasks">what to send the swarm</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Several independent jobs that can run at once: summarize N files, extract fields, classify items, translate, draft boilerplate or tests from pasted code.</li>
    <li>Well-scoped tool work in agent mode: "find every caller of X in src/api and list file:line".</li>
    <li>Not hard reasoning, design decisions, or tasks that need the conversation. Those stay with the manager.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Workers are weaker models, so the manager is told to check their output before relying
    on it. Never put secrets or credentials in a worker prompt.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">rate limits and fallback</h2>
  <p>
    Request starts are staggered to stay under z.ai rate limits. When a worker gets a
    <code>429</code>, it backs off and retries the same model, then falls back to the
    secondary model. A worker that still fails returns <code>{'{id, ok: false, error}'}</code>
    instead of breaking the whole batch.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
